 "verdict": "No pooled linear relationship between stress and lap-time loss (r=0.0428). Within drivers, the most-stressed calls sit -0.07s off the calmest ones, with 38/80 drivers slower when stressed - no better than chance (sign test p=0.7376). Stress does not appear to lead pace loss in this corpus. The largest lag correlation (lag_3, r=-0.1523) is negative, meaning higher stress preceded *faster* laps - the opposite of the hypothesis - and at p=0.1107 it is not significant once the four lags tested are accounted for.",
 "caveat": "Driver histories are namespaced per race, so no comparison spans two races. Lap-time delta is measured against each driver's own median within that race. The era confound noted in _corpus_finding.json applies here too."
